1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,720
Here's a sudden question for you.

2
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:07,740
Do you think atoms really exist?

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,400
You probably think that's obvious, right?

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,020
But actually, just a little over 120 years ago,

5
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,960
even among scientists, the idea that atoms don't exist

6
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:22,960
and are merely convenient computational tools

7
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,400
was the mainstream opinion.

8
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:31,400
Today, I'll tell you about how the mysterious motion of tiny particles released from pollen floating in water

9
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:35,040
proved the reality of atoms.

10
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,400
It's an amazing story.

11
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:44,480
Let me start by explaining the historical background.

12
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:50,400
At the end of the 19th century, the scientific world was in the midst of a great debate.

13
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,720
On one side were the atomists. Boltzmann and others argued:

14
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:12,260
Matter is made of invisible tiny particles—what we now call atoms and molecules—and heat is the motion of these molecules.

15
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,720
That's what the atomists believed.

16
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:19,360
But the opposition was also powerful.

17
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,560
The energeticists, Mach and Ostwald, countered:

18
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:28,480
Atoms are nothing but a convenient computational concept.

19
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:36,300
How can you claim something exists if you can't directly observe it?

20
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:43,260
In fact, Ernst Mach, one of the energeticists, stated plainly:

21
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:49,580
"I do not believe in atoms. They are merely an artificial convenience."

22
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:54,880
Hmm, when you think about it, their argument makes sense, doesn't it?

23
00:01:56,040 --> 00:02:00,520
Indeed, no one had ever directly seen an atom.

24
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:06,100
Going back a bit in time to 1827,

25
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,560
British botanist Robert Brown discovered a mysterious phenomenon.

26
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:17,880
When he placed pollen in water and observed it under a microscope,

27
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:23,240
tiny particles released from the pollen kept moving in zigzag patterns on their own.

28
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:25,880
As if they were alive.

29
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:29,900
Was it because the pollen was alive?

30
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:32,700
That's what Brown initially thought.

31
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:39,100
But pollen from specimen rooms over 100 years old moved the same way.

32
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:41,560
It wasn't life force.

33
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:45,500
Then was it special because it's organic matter?

34
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:51,600
He then experimented with particles of coal, rock, and metal.

35
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,260
Even particles scraped from the Sphinx showed the same behavior.

36
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:59,320
Whether it was organic or not didn't matter.

37
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:03,580
Various hypotheses were proposed afterward.

38
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:08,500
The particles moved in random directions, so it wasn't convection.

39
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:13,699
They weren't attracted to electrodes, so it wasn't electrical force.

40
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:22,680
Even after two years of observation, the motion never weakened, so it wasn't a chemical reaction like camphor boats.

41
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:31,980
For about 80 years after the 1827 discovery, no one could explain this mysterious motion.

42
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:39,660
In 1888, French physicist Louis Georges Gouy

43
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:48,500
made a crucial insight that Brownian motion couldn't be explained by the second law of thermodynamics.

44
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:57,039
Today we understand that Brownian motion doesn't contradict the second law of thermodynamics,

45
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:05,060
but indeed, "particles that keep moving forever" does sound like you could build a perpetual motion machine.

46
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:11,840
In 1905, Einstein entered the scene at age 26.

47
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:17,739
Einstein had read both Boltzmann's and Mach's books,

48
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:24,179
learned about the atomic debate, and apparently wanted to prove the existence of atoms.

49
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:32,020
That year, he published five important papers that fundamentally changed physics.

50
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:44,660
The theory of the photoelectric effect, special relativity, the famous E=mc² equation showing mass-energy equivalence,

51
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:50,540
and the main topic of today: the theory of Brownian motion.

52
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,620
Any single one of these would be a discovery worthy of history,

53
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:59,160
yet he published them all in rapid succession within one year.

54
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:05,560
1905 would later be called the "Miracle Year."

55
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:09,900
Einstein's approach was revolutionary.

56
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:17,839
The conventional approach was to try to track the movements of individual particles moving randomly.

57
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:26,740
But just tracking individual particle movements only revealed that they move irregularly—nothing more.

58
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:36,580
Einstein took a different approach: focusing on statistical behavior rather than individual movements.

59
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,740
This is similar to coin flipping.

60
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:42,640
A single result is unpredictable.

61
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:49,080
Since it's 50% heads or tails, you can only guess randomly.

62
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:55,880
But if you flip a million times, you can predict it'll be roughly half and half.

63
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,520
Similarly, while you can't know how a single particle will move in the next moment,

64
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:07,140
you should be able to predict how far it will travel over a long time.

65
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:14,000
Einstein connected Fick's law—the macroscopic law of diffusion—

66
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:21,340
with Stokes' law—the microscopic viscous resistance—through thermal energy.

67
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:26,440
And here's the equation he derived.

68
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:34,440
This was the moment when the invisible world of atoms connected with the visible motion of particles through temperature.

69
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:39,160
Einstein then had another brilliant insight.

70
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,300
Traditionally, diffusion equations described how many particles spread out.

71
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:49,500
But Einstein reinterpreted this

72
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:57,020
as a probability problem: where is a single particle most likely to be?

73
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,660
This shift in thinking was the key.

74
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:04,860
Since particles move right and left,

75
00:07:05,440 --> 00:07:08,300
the average displacement becomes zero.

76
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,460
The positives and negatives cancel out.

77
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,460
But what about the average of the squared distance?

78
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:19,860
When you square values, they always become positive,

79
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:23,820
so this time the positives and negatives don't cancel.

80
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:27,400
And here's the equation Einstein derived

81
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:32,040
for the mean squared displacement of Brownian motion.

82
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:38,800
This is amazing!

83
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:47,800
The mean squared displacement is proportional to time. Calculating specifically, the distance traveled in 30 seconds is about 4.3 micrometers.

84
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:55,840
This is large enough to be observed even with microscopes of that era. Think about it.

85
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:03,140
The size of an atom is about 0.1 to 0.3 nanometers.

86
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:10,000
Yet it became observable in the micrometer world—tens of thousands of times larger.

87
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:14,920
Einstein wrote at the end of his paper:

88
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:20,919
"I hope that a researcher will soon succeed in solving this problem."

89
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:28,160
"If this experiment succeeds, it will provide new and powerful evidence for atomic theory."

90
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:35,180
In other words, he was saying: someone good at experiments, please verify this!

91
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:43,120
The one who took up that challenge was French physicist Jean Perrin.

92
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:46,860
But the experiment was extremely difficult.

93
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:53,840
To verify the theory, the particles needed to be perfectly uniform in size.

94
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:57,260
Perrin's ingenuity was remarkable.

95
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:05,700
He created microparticles of gamboge resin, used in yellow paint, through three stages of centrifugation.

96
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:11,400
He prepared particles uniformly sized at 0.3 micrometers.

97
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:17,620
What Perrin observed was a phenomenon called sedimentation equilibrium.

98
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:22,240
Air becomes thinner at higher altitudes on mountains, right?

99
00:09:23,579 --> 00:09:27,160
This was already known in the 19th century,

100
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:32,420
and Boltzmann explained this as air molecules being pulled by Earth's gravity,

101
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:37,600
becoming denser near the surface and thinner at higher altitudes.

102
00:09:38,999 --> 00:09:41,980
But since air molecules couldn't be seen,

103
00:09:42,620 --> 00:09:45,720
his idea couldn't be proven.

104
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:52,100
However, Perrin, by observing microparticles in water under a microscope,

105
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:59,120
attempted to verify both sedimentation equilibrium and Einstein's theory of Brownian motion.

106
00:10:00,499 --> 00:10:03,600
Then came days of painstaking experiments and observations.

107
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:09,080
Tracking particles under the microscope, recording positions at regular intervals.

108
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:12,220
Repeating it again and again.

109
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:14,820
And the results?

110
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:20,220
They matched Einstein's theory perfectly!

111
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:25,000
The mean squared displacement was indeed proportional to time.

112
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:30,940
Upon learning of these results, Einstein sent Perrin a letter of gratitude.

113
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:37,560
"I had thought it would be impossible to measure Brownian motion with such precision."

114
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:42,460
"It was fortunate that you undertook this work."

115
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:47,900
Even the theorist himself thought the experiment would be too difficult,

116
00:10:48,460 --> 00:10:50,420
yet Perrin accomplished it brilliantly.

117
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:55,240
Perrin's experiments yielded something even more important.

118
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:59,800
By substituting experimental data into Einstein's equation,

119
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:02,320
the Boltzmann constant could be determined.

120
00:11:03,860 --> 00:11:08,380
And dividing the gas constant R by the Boltzmann constant gives

121
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:15,000
NA = 6.5 x 10^23

122
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:20,240
This is the number of particles in one mole of a substance—

123
00:11:20,970 --> 00:11:22,000
Avogadro's number.

124
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:30,700
Today, Avogadro's number is known to be approximately 6.02 x 10^23,

125
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:34,680
but for the time, this was an extremely accurate measurement.

126
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:41,660
Remarkably, this value matched values obtained through completely different methods.

127
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:45,860
Planck's black-body radiation, radioactive decay,

128
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:47,900
Millikan's oil-drop experiment,

129
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:54,880
X-ray diffraction—Avogadro's number obtained through various methods all agreed.

130
00:11:56,399 --> 00:11:59,339
This can't be explained as coincidence, can it?

131
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:07,660
In 1909, Ostwald, who had previously denied atoms, conceded:

132
00:12:09,380 --> 00:12:16,400
"I am now convinced that we have recently obtained experimental proof of the atomic hypothesis."

133
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:21,740
"I no longer have any reason to oppose the atomic hypothesis."

134
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:27,980
In 1926, Perrin was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics.

135
00:12:28,940 --> 00:12:36,460
The citation: "For his work on the discontinuous structure of matter, especially his discovery of sedimentation equilibrium."

136
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:45,820
The discontinuous structure of matter refers to the idea that matter is composed of tiny particles called atoms.

137
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:51,679
The atomic debate ended with the victory of atomism.

138
00:12:52,820 --> 00:13:00,200
But we shouldn't mock the energeticists as stubborn people who refused to accept new ideas.

139
00:13:01,199 --> 00:13:08,340
They were simply being faithful to Occam's razor—that we shouldn't assume things we cannot observe.

140
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,000
Their attitude was scientifically correct.

141
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:19,959
What made Einstein and Perrin remarkable was that they made the invisible visible.

142
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:25,540
They demonstrated the reality of atoms through methods previously thought impossible.

143
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:33,600
Brownian motion, discovered in 1827,

144
00:13:34,190 --> 00:13:39,380
was the mysterious movement of microparticles that remained a mystery for about 80 years.

145
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:43,640
Einstein was the one who explained it through theory.

146
00:13:44,940 --> 00:13:47,119
Using statistical mechanics,

147
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:52,220
he built a theory connecting visible particles to invisible atoms.

148
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:57,420
And Perrin proved the theory through tenacious experimentation,

149
00:13:58,220 --> 00:14:00,980
putting an end to the atomic debate.

150
00:14:02,259 --> 00:14:06,220
The existence of atoms, which we take for granted today,

151
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:10,860
required the accumulation of many people's efforts and ingenuity

152
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:15,939
before it became a scientifically grounded, empirically proven reality.

153
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:19,560
Making the invisible visible.

154
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:25,600
More recent examples include neutrino oscillations and gravitational waves.

155
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:30,780
I hope this conveys some of the romance of science.

156
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:35,060
Until next time, take care!
